% Reachability over the module graph, and the cycles it finds. Split out of
% `modgraph.dl` because it is the one thing there that is not linear: a
% transitive closure over the import graph, quadratic in the graph's density.
% Import it when you want cycles or reachability; import `modgraph.dl` alone
% when you want `dep`, `runtime_dep`, `unit_dep`, `package_edge` or
% `external_dep`, and pay nothing for this.
%
%   file_reaches(A, B)       a chain of dep leads from A to B
%   in_cycle(F)              F is on an import cycle
%   cycle_edge(A, B)         a dep edge that lies on a cycle
%
% **This follows `dep`, which is every import, type-only included.** A cycle it
% reports may not exist at run time; re-derive over `runtime_dep` before calling
% one real (`../SCHEMA.md`, and the language reference's first trap).
%
% **Cost.** On a 156k-line project this is seconds. On Grafana's frontend —
% 8,910 files, 56,047 imports — `file_reaches` is **17.45M derived pairs** and
% the program holding it peaks at 12.6 GB; the same graph's *runtime* closure is
% 11.83M pairs. Over a few thousand files, seed the question instead of closing
% over the whole graph (`callreach_seeded.dl` is the idiom), or ask it of a
% subtree.

import "modgraph.dl".

file_reaches(A, B) :- dep(A, B).
file_reaches(A, C) :- dep(A, B), file_reaches(B, C).
in_cycle(F) :- file_reaches(F, F).
cycle_edge(A, B) :- dep(A, B), file_reaches(B, A).
